[{:seon.task/name "roots-procs-are-the-first-forks-graph"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: flow. The owner's design, confirmed 2026-11-16 (decisions: the system process is the fork point): the binary's main process is the system, single-threaded for life; it loads main by load-diff! of its held db against each commit notice, collects, and forks. Root is its first fork; agents and test children are root's siblings and inherit only what the system process built once. Bound: a forked agent's own memory <=5 MB, no baggage (docs/cost.md#the-test-systems-lower-bound); every READY gives a fresh agent's private bytes after fork, after a collection, after one read, beside 5 MB. Flow: root's procs as the first fork's graph, started in root, none in the system process; the commit stream to the system process as one ordered Flow path, a drain answered. Flow's answer to runtime is still owed."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-throwing-step-holds-its-message-and-parks",
             :plan
             "Reproduction (e3_failure E3.1): a throwing step loses its message (flow/impl.clj proc catch), keeps its old state and goes on; the only record is a sliding(100) error chan. With the hold pattern (e3 `held`, ~25 lines) the proc keeps the message, emits a :failed fact with the cause, and narrows ::flow/input-filter to nothing, so its producers park by back-pressure and its consumers park on empty inputs (E3.2: no crash, no timeout, nothing dropped). On the load effect's one broadcast after a reload (a ::reloaded signal, flow's own signal-select) it retries the held message through the Var; E3.3: the fix ran the held message and the 8 queued behind it, in order, with no other intervention. Correct: seon.step/held (or the same in flow's proc loop), used by every root proc; my.fx's load owner casts ::reloaded with the vars it changed. A request a held proc took is answered when it completes, never with a fake fallback (root's api does this for a throwing step: docs/flow.md).",
             :kind :work,
             :proofs
             #{seon.step-test/a-reload-retries-the-held-message
               seon.step-test/a-throw-holds-the-message-and-records-the-cause}}
