 {:seon.task/name "every-seon-proc-follows-one-flow-standard-with-a-runnable-example-each"
  :seon.task/plan "Goal: one standard every Seon proc follows, written once in docs/flow.md (stays at most 110 lines, shorter), each pattern with a small runnable example that is a test, so it stays true; plus an inventory of what should be a Flow proc and is not, each filed as a task for its owner. Root approved 2026-11-13; context indexes the examples so an agent touching a pattern is shown them. What exists: docs/flow.md (110 lines), web-research/flow-source.md (upstream against Jolt, line by line), deps' Flow gaps A-E, and port names in use (:to-api x7, :in x5, :main x4, fifteen one-offs). Standard: (1) naming: in-ports named for what arrives, out-ports :to-<pid> when feeding one proc else named for what leaves, replies on the asker's :answers, one key for a reply address and :seon.bound/bound for a message's bound; (2) lifecycle: init takes nothing from outside but in-ports/out-ports, pause and resume idempotent, stop releases everything and answers every open request :stopped, a transition's throw is reported with its command, pause leaves in-flight messages in buffers [M]; (3) patterns each with an example: request/reply by rid, completion as close, fan-out, fan-in, back-pressure, pipelines (lift1->step), deadlines as messages, effects; (4) not allowed: atom as proc state, thread outside declared edges, blocking take in a step, poll, clock read, FIFO-position answers. Measured at the REPL on Jolt, n at most 4 [M]: rid replies match; a throwing step does not stop the proc; an output to a missing coord gives no error, only ::flow/undeliverable on the report chan; a closed in-port yields one nil in order; a deadline via a/timeout onto the own in-port arrives as a message; an open request at stop is not answered by Flow, it is the owner's job. Examples: test/seon/flow_patterns_test.clj, one deftest per pattern, 15-25 lines, ~8 tests ~5M ticks, red first under a mutation. Inventory tasks: the test child's link (second EDN codec, a guard turning undecodable into a value, a pid stamped in the decoder) is built first after the standard: one codec jfasl, an unreadable value as the link's typed error, fan-in knowing its senders; the agent's state and link atoms (agent.clj:36, 41); await-reply's promise table (agent.clj:235); the 7888 server's accept thread (server.clj:633); tree-runs atom (system.clj:2126); server's index-now atom (server.clj:69). Left alone: fork_server loop, fx read-lines! thread, intr and uncaught atoms (edges), caches (values kept by input). Survey (runtime, the Flow specialist, 2026-11-14; notes runtime-flow-survey-2026-11-14.md) [M]: the standard is one pattern per need: a step-fn Var with schema'd state; a conn for a dependency; seon.step/put-after! on the proc's own in-port for any wait past a bound (never (timeout ms) in a step: docs/flow.md's retry idiom goes); pend/ask/answered with one :seon.step/request-id for a proc's ask; ask-edge for an edge's; seon.fx owner-proc and worker-proc per effect kind; one edge reader thread per blocking read; run-loop for a process that must fork. Found duplicated: three request-id schemes for one pattern (step/ask's :rid and :tag, fx's :dest {:agent :op :k}, the agent's :k from :tx-seq): one key, :seon.step/request-id (the schema has it), and one asker helper; owners of system, prove, render, reconcile and fx review. docs/flow.md's Idioms becomes the standard table, each row with its runnable example (the proof this task names). Design guards (the owner, 2026-11-14: proofs that prevent bad designs, not only tests of a fix), each a bin/laws law like clocks and measures, refusing a site the primer does not name with its reason: (1) buffers: every sliding-buffer and dropping-buffer in src is named in docs/flow.md#channel-idioms with the one stream whose newest value subsumes the rest (found 2026-11-14: a sliding buffer carrying many branches' notices was agreed before review caught it); (2) threads: every Thread., future, a/thread and a/io-thread in src is named as an edge reader for a blocking read or a :mixed worker (found: seon.repl/serve-item!'s two io-threads per item, the exit watch's io-thread per child); (3) unbounded waits: every deref of a promise or blocking take with no bound in src is named as an edge whose producer always answers (found: fx!'s await-reply with no bound, the model call's hidden stall). The primer is one page read whole (docs/flow.md, core-async merged in 7de4e6d47e), and a running graph takes new code and procs with no restart (seon.step-test/a-running-graph-takes-new-code-and-new-procs-with-no-restart). Refinement (the owner, 2026-11-14: \"it doesn't seem like the design is that refined. Why do we have three io only procs?\"; plan in notes runtime-flow-refinement-plan-2026-11-14.md, against upstream's \"communication-free functions\"): (1) the agent's three procs exist because steps block ([M] repl.clj:1303-1475: :repl runs a whole work! in one step waiting on promises :transport delivers; :tests runs a whole test run in one step): a turn becomes a step (the model call sent with fx-send!, its outcome a message, the next step runs the reply's evals), evals stay synchronous with their declared bound, :tests goes (a test run and an item each a test-run-step proc added by update!), leaving :transport (the socket's router) and :repl; deletes tests-step, serve-item!'s io-threads, work!'s in-step loop and :draining. (2) Flow's hazards at source in our Jolt Flow: control put with offer! and close outside the graph lock (no wedge), no default clocks (compute, ping, stop declared by the caller; finish-outputs folded into stop's one deadline), the report chan piped into root's :back as problems (Seon reads it nowhere today [M], so undeliverable outputs are lost past 100); casts unused [M], explained. (3) docs/flow.md keeps four bounded warnings (an eval inside its step, a mult's slowest tap, a single-stream sliding drop, a changed proc's restart); the rest become impossible by fix or law. Order 90/10: report pipe, declared clocks, control fix and :tests as per-run procs first; the turn as a step with context-4 after the merge slice."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-lossy-buffer-the-flow-primer-does-not-name-is-refused seon.check-test/a-thread-outside-a-proc-the-flow-primer-does-not-name-is-refused seon.check-test/an-unbounded-wait-the-flow-primer-does-not-name-is-refused seon.step-test/a-running-graph-takes-new-code-and-new-procs-with-no-restart}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.lib/a-process-the-binary-starts-runs-as-production"}}

 {:seon.task/name "every-wait-past-a-bound-is-a-flow-message-from-the-one-timer"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-11-14: \"No timers. We must take anything that needs a timer and port the code to work with flow.\" The one Flow-native clock is seon.step/put-after!: a deadline delivered as a message on a proc's own channel, taken by its step (with seon.step/timer, arm! and fired! for a self-timer). Nothing that times things is removed before its Flow replacement does the same job, and every bound stays a spec'd config entry with its reason (AGENTS §3, §5). Inventory [M, read 2026-11-14 at 3d1b134c7b: bin/laws clocks's 19 sites and a scan of bin/]. On put-after! already, kept: seon.cron/cron-proc (the schedule); seon.fx/wake-later and refill (the delayed insert); seon.agent/await-reply (fx!'s non-streamed bound, :seon.config.fx/bounds); seon.fx/stream-due (a stream's bound of quiet); seon.agent/arm-wall! (a test's wall bound, folded into the effects owner's request bound with tests' :process kind). To port, each with its replacement: (1) root's :stall-check poll (seon.system api-proc, system.clj:934 and :1061) and the agent proc's :stall? with its effect exemption (agent_proc.clj:122): each request's own bound armed by put-after! when the agent proc takes it, the same {:op :stalled}, :seon.agent/stalled fact, stall-task evidence and open problem; :seon.config.agent/stall-ms stays as the default bound (runtime, the first slice, task seon.agent-proc/a-stalled-eval-ends-with-its-error); (2) seon.system/stall-state's Flow ping bound (:seon.config.check/ping-ms): the ping's answer as messages, a proc mid-step past its bound a put-after! due on the asker; (3) bin/bounded and seon.proofs/step-bounds (a check step's whole run), bin/stalls (a test past its end bound), bin/check's 600 s overran watchdog (bin/check:390), bin/agent-checks' `timeout 300`: each a :process request with its declared :seon.bound/ms on the effects owner, past it bin/evidence and the group kill (tests' :process kind with runtime's deadline); (4) bin/jolt-gates' `timeout -k 30 1100 make`: a gate run as a :process request with its bound (deps, once the effects owner runs gates); (5) bin/held's MS (root's own stop, :seon.config.root/stop-ms): the stop of the process that holds the timer cannot be timed by it; kept as the boot/edge exception unless the online takeover's new root times the old one's end with put-after! (runtime, Stage 3). Measurement windows, not decisions, kept and named in docs/clocks.md: seon.check's idle, scale-work and ping windows, bin/evidence's 1 s between samples, bin/worktrees -w's display loop. Proofs, red first, one per port: a request past its bound is answered by its owner from a put-after! due with no poll running (seon.agent-proc-test/a-request-past-its-bound-is-stalled-with-no-poll), and one per bin/ script as it becomes a :process request (tests). Estimate: one put-after! per request (~1k ticks), no proc woken while nothing is due (the idle check holds it). Builders: runtime (1, 2, 5), tests (3), deps (4). Root's ruling (2026-11-14): (5) bin/held's MS stays only as the stop's edge exception until the online takeover, whose landing deletes it: the new root's Flow proc times the old root's stop with put-after! (task seon.system/a-stop-or-crash-resumes-from-one-resume-point-and-says-what-it-lost). The measurement windows stay (no decision). (3) is tests', (4) deps', each sent its rows. Update (deps, 2026-11-14): bin/jolt-gates' `timeout` is gone in deps' fork-bound pin; each gate runs under bin/bounded (its own group, evidence past the bound; bin/bounded ends its group when it is itself ended). So (4) retires bin/bounded's role for gates, joining (3)'s port of bin/bounded. The owner (2026-11-14, root b93545da1f): once Flow runs, only Flow-based systems hold state, and an agent specializes: runtime, the Flow specialist, owns every port in this task, (3) and (4) included (bin/bounded, step-bounds, bin/stalls, check's watchdog, agent-checks, gates under bin/bounded); tests and deps review their files."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-proc-test/a-request-past-its-bound-is-stalled-with-no-poll}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "every-request-is-answered-at-each-end-of-its-holder"
  :seon.task/plan "flow, 2026-11-15, root's order: spine 1 of notes/flow-collapse-plan-2026-11-15.md (one way to ask and be answered). What exists [M, git grep at jolt/flow]: seon.step/pend, ask and answered are the asker's side, used by the reconciler, render, prove, fx, vector and root's api (14 asks). The answerer's side has no one mechanism. Root's api keeps a rid table (seon.system/pend); the agent proc keeps :inflight, released by any of its ends since d8d209489c; an agent keeps :waits for await-reply (9 calls); seon.system/await-db keeps preds; the reconciler keeps :waits and :judged; ask-edge puts a promise and leaves its end to the proc. Every hang this week was an entry one of those tables released only on success (:holding, :judged, a peer's read, clone!'s await-reply). The design (90/10, no new mechanism): (1) the answerer's contract, docs/flow.md#the-five-rules rule 3: a proc that holds a request owes exactly one answer on every path; (2) seon.step/end-all, (st, why) -> [st' answers]: every entry the proc holds answered with the typed :seon.step/ended {:why} (a stop, its in-port's EOF, a step's defect, a declared bound) and its table emptied; each holder calls it at each of its ends: its ::flow/stop transition, the EOF of the port its requests came on, its step-error edge; (3) the holders converted onto it, one area a landing: the agent's await-reply (:waits -> the transport proc's pending, answered by end-all at root's EOF or stop), root's api rid table and agent-proc's :inflight (their release-by-any-end code becomes the one call), the reconciler's :waits/:judged (its stop transition calls end-all). The law :unended-wait (web-2, flow's definition) finds the remaining sites. Estimate: a pending entry is an assoc, O(1) ~1 us; end-all is O(pending) once an end; ~30 lines plus ~3 a holder converted; src net negative. Deletes: each holder's own end-answering code (agent-proc's end-hold paths, the reconciler's stop doseqs, await-reply's finally) -> end-all; :waits -> pend. No functionality removed: every request is still answered, now on every path. Proofs, red first: seon.step-test/every-pending-request-is-answered-at-each-end (a proc holding 3 requests answers all 3 :seon.step/ended at its stop, at its in-port's EOF and at a step's throw; red today: the throw path answers none); seon.agent-test/an-await-reply-ends-when-root-goes (an eval waiting on root's answer is answered :seon.step/ended when root's end closes; red today: it waits until the process ends).\nINSTANCES, 2026-11-15 [M]: (a) data: root's agent proc step threw on an agent's hello (agent-frame building a refused contract error from :load-errors), its state was kept and the frame dropped, so the spawn waiting on that hello was never answered: spawn! waited 887 s, until root stopped (agent c4e03df303b3b556, :last-frame-ms nil, result/a567040a; the throw result/c8810c0e). A step that throws must answer what waits on its input (the spawn with the error) or end what it holds (end-all on the step's own failure), never drop it. (b) flow: seon.server/serve-connection!'s @(c/effect! sys :server-op ...) has no end when the owner loses the answer (task seon.server/an-eval-at-root-always-answers-its-asker). One rule covers both: every wait held by a proc ends at its owner's answer, the owner's typed refusal, the owner's failure, or root's stop.\nAPPROVED (root, 2026-11-15, gap 1; plan notes flow-every-request-answered-plan-2026-11-15.md): a defect in an agent's proc is that agent's end, by the one path a refused spawn takes. In the api's :error arm (system.clj:921-945), when the pid is one of :agent-procs, call spawn-failed (system.clj:374): it records the error on the agent, answers its spawn, and sends it {:op :failed}, so it hangs up and its :ended runs end-agent, which answers every in-flight request. Nothing new; the :reconciler-defect special case stays (the reconciler isn't an agent). Proof red first: seon.problems-test/a-defect-on-an-agents-frame-ends-the-agent-and-answers-its-spawn (one api step on an :error from an agent's pid; the spawn's promise is realized with :seon.agent/failed and the defect as its cause, and [:a1 :in] gets {:op :failed}). Prepared in /tmp/flow-gap1/apply.py; builds after messaging part 1 lands. COST: O(1) a defect, as a refused spawn costs. Gap 2 (seon_eval hangs) waits for evidence: runs/root.log.prev now keeps the last start's log (bin/system, in part 1's land). Gap 3 (an op sent while root starts): red-first test next. GAP 1 BUILT (flow, jolt/flow c29675b251): the api's :error arm runs spawn-failed for an agent's pid; proof seon.problems-test/a-defect-on-an-agents-frame-ends-the-agent-and-answers-its-spawn, red first (the spawn unanswered, its deref a deadlock Jolt named), then green; problems-test 77/77. Gap 3 (an op sent while root starts) and gap 2's evidence (root.log.prev and kept shared-root logs, landed 5ea1bbd49e) remain."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.step-test/every-pending-request-is-answered-at-each-end seon.agent-test/an-await-reply-ends-when-root-goes}}
]
